          "type": "output_text",
          "annotations": [],
          "logprobs": [],
          "text": "{\"occurrence_id\":\"flaubert-madame-bovary-a6b28fb94009e312\",\"core_classification\":{\"label_support\":{\"truth_conditional\":3,\"performative\":4,\"exclamatory_reflexive\":0,\"other\":0},\"confidence\":0.97,\"analysis\":\"Emma’s future-tense “je t’aimerai” presents her continuing love as assured, supporting T, but in this sequence of pledges—being everything to Rodolphe, serving as family and homeland, and caring for him—it primarily enacts a relational undertaking, strongly supporting P. Nothing indicates that the words escape reflexively or function as affective discharge independently of their avowal-and-promise force.\",\"ambiguity\":\"The future form can be heard both as a confident prediction of continued feeling and as a promise; the immediate parallel commitments make the performative reading stronger.\"},\"other_diagnosis\":{\"tpe_failure\":null,\"core_not_context\":null},\"utterance_status\":{\"status\":\"direct\",\"description\":\"Emma directly addresses Rodolphe during their conversation about their planned elopement and future life together.\"},\"contextual_interpretation\":\"Emma uses the utterance to reassure Rodolphe about what he is supposedly sacrificing and to promise that she will replace his existing attachments through comprehensive care and love. Her idealization, emotional intensity, and Rodolphe’s apparent reluctance shape the scene, but they do not create independent expressive/reflexive force in the core words.\",\"evidence\":[{\"evidence_id\":\"e1\",\"source\":\"local_text\",\"quotation_or_description\":\"“je te soignerai, je t’aimerai” (“I shall care for you, I shall love you”).\",\"supports\":\"The coordinated future-tense clauses frame loving as one of Emma’s promised forms of continuing care, strongly supporting P and also supporting T.\",\"confidence\":0.99},{\"evidence_id\":\"e2\",\"source\":\"local_text\",\"quotation_or_description\":\"“je serai tout pour toi, je te serai une famille, une patrie” (“I shall be everything to you; I shall be a family to you, a homeland”).\",\"supports\":\"The surrounding sequence is an expansive undertaking to assume an enduring relational role, strengthening the performative interpretation of “je t’aimerai.”\",\"confidence\":0.98},{\"evidence_id\":\"e3\",\"source\":\"local_text\",\"quotation_or_description\":\"Emma says this while discussing Rodolphe’s leaving his attachments and life for their planned flight together.\",\"supports\":\"The utterance answers a perceived concern about sacrifice and functions as reassurance through commitment, rather than as a reflex-like verbal discharge.\",\"confidence\":0.96},{\"evidence_id\":\"e4\",\"source\":\"supplied_metadata\",\"quotation_or_description\":\"The occurrence is tagged as affirmative future tense and as a valid explicit first-person love-to-you expression.\",\"supports\":\"Confirms the future-oriented form that permits both predictive/avowal force and promissory force.\",\"confidence\":0.99}],\"background_knowledge\":{\"used\":false,\"familiarity\":\"none\",\"confidence\":null,\"contribution\":null},\"ontology_assessment\":{\"fit\":\"natural\",\"diagnosis\":\"T and P naturally capture the utterance’s combined assurance of future love and enactment of a relational promise. Emotional and narrative complexity remains contextual, with no residual core-force pressure requiring O.\",\"candidate_recurrent_dimension\":null},\"notes\":null}"
        }
      ],
